1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,640
 Every time your browser or your app

2
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:04,600
 sends a request to a server,

3
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:06,860
 the server sends back a response.

4
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:08,340
 And the first line of that response

5
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:10,280
 as a three digit number.

6
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:12,780
 The number is the HTTP status code.

7
00:00:12,780 --> 00:00:15,000
 In this case here, 200 is the status code.

8
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,580
 Okay is the reason, reason phase, scratch that.

9
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:19,120
 Okay is the reason phase.

10
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:22,360
 The job of the status code is to tell you what happened

11
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:24,240
 while the body gives you the detail.

12
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:25,520
 And you can already tell a lot

13
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:28,080
 from the first digit of the number.

14
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:30,960
 ACTB status scores are grouped into five classes.

15
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:33,120
 The first digit of the three digit number

16
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:34,800
 tells you which class it is.

17
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:38,200
 The easiest way to think of them is to think of

18
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:40,280
 asking for a book at a bookshop counter.

19
00:00:40,280 --> 00:00:43,040
 One XX means, hold on, I'm working on it.

20
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:44,340
 Like the attendant telling you,

21
00:00:44,340 --> 00:00:45,920
 hey, give me a second, let me check.

22
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:47,600
 Two XX means success.

23
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:49,200
 Hey, I've got your back.

24
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:50,040
 Yeah, it is.

25
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:51,000
 I've got your book, right?

26
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:52,080
 Yeah, it is.

27
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:54,240
 And three XX means redirection.

28
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:57,360
 We moved that group to our Other branch, go there.

29
00:00:57,360 --> 00:00:58,980
 4xx means client error.

30
00:00:58,980 --> 00:01:01,100
 Something is wrong with what you asked for.

31
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:02,740
 Maybe you asked for a book that doesn't exist

32
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:04,900
 or you didn't show your Bookshop membership card.

33
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:06,500
 5xx means server error.

34
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:09,180
 Your request was fine, but something broke on our end.

35
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:12,240
 Our bookstore computer system went down, whatever it is.

36
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:14,300
 So when something fails or succeeds,

37
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:17,140
 look at the first digit of this three-digit number first.

38
00:01:17,140 --> 00:01:18,580
 If we start with a four,

39
00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:20,740
 we start by checking the request the client sends.

40
00:01:20,740 --> 00:01:23,140
 If we start with a five, we start by checking the server.

41
00:01:23,140 --> 00:01:24,380
 Now we'll go through each of them,

42
00:01:24,380 --> 00:01:26,500
 each of this group using our bookstore API.

43
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:30,100
 The 1XX status codes, you rarely see this for yourself.

44
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:32,420
 You might see 101 Switching Protocols

45
00:01:32,420 --> 00:01:35,380
 when a connection upgrades to a WebSocket,

46
00:01:35,380 --> 00:01:40,060
 but your day-to-day API is mostly 2XX to 5XX HTTP status

47
00:01:40,060 --> 00:01:40,780
 codes.

48
00:01:40,780 --> 00:01:43,580
 The 2XX status codes mean success.

49
00:01:43,580 --> 00:01:46,820
 And there are three that you will use all of the time.

50
00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:49,060
 200 OK means your request work.

51
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:50,020
 Yeah, it's the result.

52
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:53,780
 So getBooks42 returns 200 OK with the book.

53
00:01:53,780 --> 00:01:56,060
 201 created means the request world

54
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:57,380
 and something new was created.

55
00:01:57,380 --> 00:01:59,960
 So when you POST books to add a new book,

56
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:01,880
 the right answer is 201.

57
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:04,320
 You can also add a Location header that points to the new book.

58
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:07,720
 204, no content, means that your request work,

59
00:02:07,720 --> 00:02:09,240
 but there's nothing to send back.

60
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:11,680
 Usually we get a 204 a lot with delete.

61
00:02:11,680 --> 00:02:13,120
 For example, in delete book 42,

62
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:14,560
 there is nothing left to show.

63
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:17,760
 So you return 204 with an empty body.

64
00:02:17,760 --> 00:02:21,000
 The three ISX ATTV status codes are redirect.

65
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,040
 they tell the clients that what it wants

66
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:27,880
 is at a different address.

67
00:02:27,880 --> 00:02:29,960
 So the new address goes in the location header.

68
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:32,160
 There are four common three access HTTP status code

69
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:33,220
 that you will see.

70
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:34,060
 And to pick the right one,

71
00:02:34,060 --> 00:02:35,200
 you just need to answer two questions.

72
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:37,720
 Question one, is the move permanent or temporary?

73
00:02:37,720 --> 00:02:41,440
 If a book shop moves that book to other branch for good,

74
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:43,520
 that's a 301 or a 308.

75
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:45,640
 If the book is only there for now and it's coming back,

76
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:47,440
 that's a 302 or 307.

77
00:02:47,440 --> 00:02:50,440
 The second question, does the request stay the same

78
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:52,260
 when the client follows the redirect.

79
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:54,940
 So you come to the counter to add in a header form,

80
00:02:54,940 --> 00:02:57,820
 and they send it to the other branch, which 307 or 308.

81
00:02:57,820 --> 00:02:59,900
 You take the same form to the other branch,

82
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:01,060
 and you add it in there.

83
00:03:01,060 --> 00:03:03,480
 With 301 and 302, the browser is allowed

84
00:03:03,480 --> 00:03:05,080
 to switch from POST to GET.

85
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:08,060
 So for normal web pages, 301 or 302 are fine.

86
00:03:08,060 --> 00:03:12,460
 You're redirecting an API where post 301 and 302 are fine.

87
00:03:12,460 --> 00:03:13,300
 Scratch that.

88
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:15,380
 So for normal web pages, 301 and 302 are fine.

89
00:03:15,380 --> 00:03:18,060
 If you're redirecting an API where post requests matter,

90
00:03:18,060 --> 00:03:20,540
 I will use 307 and 308.

91
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:23,340
 Now the 4xx status codes are client errors.

92
00:03:23,340 --> 00:03:25,140
 They mean the system thinks something is wrong

93
00:03:25,140 --> 00:03:27,380
 with the request and they are the ones

94
00:03:27,380 --> 00:03:28,460
 you would debug the most.

95
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:31,140
 Let's start with 400 and 422 because they sound similar.

96
00:03:31,140 --> 00:03:32,980
 400 by request means the server can't read

97
00:03:32,980 --> 00:03:34,140
 your request properly.

98
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:36,180
 Like broken JSON with a missing bracket.

99
00:03:36,180 --> 00:03:38,700
 422 Unprocessable Content means that the server

100
00:03:38,700 --> 00:03:40,260
 could read the request just fine,

101
00:03:40,260 --> 00:03:41,940
 but the data inside does not make sense.

102
00:03:41,940 --> 00:03:45,140
 If you send a bookstore a new book with no title

103
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:47,180
 or something like that, you get 422.

104
00:03:47,180 --> 00:03:50,080
 Some API send 400 for validation errors too.

105
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:52,620
 I think 4.2.2 is clearer because it tells the front end

106
00:03:52,620 --> 00:03:55,700
 that the format was fine and data is what needs fixing.

107
00:03:55,700 --> 00:03:58,100
 404 not found means that there's nothing in the address.

108
00:03:58,100 --> 00:04:01,900
 So if I send gets book 9999 and there's no book 9999,

109
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:02,740
 the book does not exist.

110
00:04:02,740 --> 00:04:05,100
 So this client gets a 404 not found.

111
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:06,620
 You probably send a 404 yourself.

112
00:04:06,620 --> 00:04:09,500
 410 gone is a stronger version of 404.

113
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:11,220
 It means that this resource was here,

114
00:04:11,220 --> 00:04:13,220
 it was removed on purpose and it should not be back.

115
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:16,300
 Like a book that you've taken out of the catalog for good.

116
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:21,140
 In practice, most APIs use 404, but Google's docs say it treats 404

117
00:04:21,140 --> 00:04:22,700
 and 410 the same way.

118
00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:25,460
 So you use 410 gone when you want to be clear that something

119
00:04:25,460 --> 00:04:26,580
 is gone for good.

120
00:04:26,580 --> 00:04:29,660
 409 conflict means the request clashed

121
00:04:29,660 --> 00:04:32,300
 with the current state of the thing that you're changing.

122
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:34,840
 Say two admins open book 42 at the same time.

123
00:04:34,840 --> 00:04:36,300
 Admin A change the price and save.

124
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:38,100
 Admin B is still looking at the old version.

125
00:04:38,100 --> 00:04:39,780
 They change the title and save.

126
00:04:39,780 --> 00:04:41,660
 And that save will put the old price back.

127
00:04:41,660 --> 00:04:43,820
 So if the API keeps track of version,

128
00:04:43,820 --> 00:04:46,480
 It can reply with 409 conflicts to admin B instead,

129
00:04:46,480 --> 00:04:48,300
 so that it can reload and try again.

130
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:49,800
 429, Too Many Requests means

131
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:50,940
 you have been rate limited.

132
00:04:50,940 --> 00:04:53,700
 It means you are sent too many requests in a short time

133
00:04:53,700 --> 00:04:55,200
 and is asking you to slow down.

134
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:57,140
 Now, 401 versus 403.

135
00:04:57,140 --> 00:04:59,380
 401 versus 403 are two status codes

136
00:04:59,380 --> 00:05:01,660
 that people mix up together a lot.

137
00:05:01,660 --> 00:05:05,540
 Say our bookstore has an admin route, delete book 42.

138
00:05:05,540 --> 00:05:07,020
 Only admins can delete book.

139
00:05:07,020 --> 00:05:08,500
 Case one, now let's look at two scenarios.

140
00:05:08,500 --> 00:05:10,620
 Case one, a request comes in with no token at all,

141
00:05:10,620 --> 00:05:11,460
 or the token has expired.

142
00:05:11,460 --> 00:05:13,780
 The server has no idea what you're sending this request.

143
00:05:13,780 --> 00:05:16,160
 That is a 401 unauthorized.

144
00:05:16,160 --> 00:05:17,840
 Case two, the request has a valid token.

145
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:19,560
 So the server knows exactly where it is.

146
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:21,680
 It's a customer, and customers cannot delete book.

147
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:23,040
 That is a 403 forbidden.

148
00:05:23,040 --> 00:05:24,960
 So two different cases, right?

149
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:26,760
 It's like when you have an hotel keycard.

150
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:28,720
 401 is when you don't even have a keycard at all

151
00:05:28,720 --> 00:05:29,800
 or your card doesn't work.

152
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:31,540
 403 is when your card works fine,

153
00:05:31,540 --> 00:05:32,520
 but it's for another room

154
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:33,800
 and you're trying to enter another room.

155
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:35,400
 So 401 is kind of who are you?

156
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:37,240
 While 403 is kind of, I know who you are,

157
00:05:37,240 --> 00:05:38,480
 but you can't do this.

158
00:05:38,480 --> 00:05:40,240
 The product of business people mostly is the name.

159
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:42,000
 401 is called unauthorized.

160
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,720
 And meanwhile, it's really about authentication.

161
00:05:44,720 --> 00:05:47,960
 403, on the other hand, is the one that is about permission.

162
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:50,840
 On a 401, the right move is to send the user to login

163
00:05:50,840 --> 00:05:52,120
 or refresh their token.

164
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:54,320
 On a 403, logging in again won't help

165
00:05:54,320 --> 00:05:56,320
 because the server already knows who they are.

166
00:05:56,320 --> 00:05:58,160
 You just need to show them a message that they don't have

167
00:05:58,160 --> 00:05:59,400
 the access to do this.

168
00:05:59,400 --> 00:06:02,920
 Sometimes a server will return 404 instead of 403 on purpose

169
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:04,760
 just to hide that something exists.

170
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:06,600
 For example, GitHub's API does this.

171
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:07,960
 If you ask for a private repository

172
00:06:07,960 --> 00:06:10,800
 that you don't have access to, you get a 404

173
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:12,980
 so that GitHub doesn't confirm to you

174
00:06:12,980 --> 00:06:14,540
 that the repository exists.

175
00:06:14,540 --> 00:06:15,880
 You shouldn't have access to it,

176
00:06:15,880 --> 00:06:18,240
 and you shouldn't even know that it exists.

177
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:21,000
 Now, the 5xx status codes are server errors.

178
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:24,440
 The requests look fine, but the server failed to handle it.

179
00:06:24,440 --> 00:06:27,720
 500 internal server error is the general one.

180
00:06:27,720 --> 00:06:29,160
 Something unexpected went wrong.

181
00:06:29,160 --> 00:06:31,960
 Usually, it's an exception in your code

182
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:33,160
 that nothing caught.

183
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:36,280
 In most real setup, a request first hits a proxy,

184
00:06:36,280 --> 00:06:38,880
 like an NGINX or a load balancer,

185
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:40,960
 and the proxy passes it to your app.

186
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:44,120
 502 bad gateway means that the proxy

187
00:06:44,120 --> 00:06:46,300
 got an invalid response from your app

188
00:06:46,300 --> 00:06:48,680
 or couldn't get a proper response from it at all.

189
00:06:48,680 --> 00:06:51,240
 504 gateway timeout means that the proxy

190
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:54,520
 waited for your app, and your app took too long to answer.

191
00:06:54,520 --> 00:06:57,320
 A very slow database query can actually cause this one.

192
00:06:57,320 --> 00:07:00,360
 503 service unavailable means the server cannot handle requests

193
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:03,400
 at the moment, and that situation is temporary.

194
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:04,620
 It might be overlooked.

195
00:07:04,620 --> 00:07:05,960
 It might be that the server is overloaded,

196
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:07,900
 or maybe the server is down for maintenance.

197
00:07:07,900 --> 00:07:11,020
 And there are some common HTTP status code mistakes

198
00:07:11,020 --> 00:07:12,500
 in API that people make.

199
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:15,880
 The first one, common one, is returning 200 OK for everything.

200
00:07:15,880 --> 00:07:18,460
 If your request fails and your API still returns 200 OK,

201
00:07:18,460 --> 00:07:20,460
 with something like error or something went wrong

202
00:07:20,460 --> 00:07:22,780
 inside the body, your front end only sees 200.

203
00:07:22,780 --> 00:07:25,340
 Your front end sees 200 and is like, OK, yeah, book saved.

204
00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:27,740
 Your monitoring also counts it as a success.

205
00:07:27,740 --> 00:07:30,380
 So you need to send the actual status code

206
00:07:30,380 --> 00:07:32,100
 and then put the details in the body.

207
00:07:32,100 --> 00:07:34,480
 The second mistake that people make with HTTP status code

208
00:07:34,480 --> 00:07:37,660
 is using 500 for things that the clients got wrong.

209
00:07:37,660 --> 00:07:40,500
 For example, say someone says, get book 9999,

210
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:42,860
 and you use find instead of find or fill.

211
00:07:42,860 --> 00:07:44,180
 So the book comes back as null,

212
00:07:44,180 --> 00:07:46,620
 and then your code tries to read the title from null,

213
00:07:46,620 --> 00:07:49,360
 and then it crashes, and its clients get to 500.

214
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:51,900
 The right answer is a 404, not a 500,

215
00:07:51,900 --> 00:07:53,580
 because the book does not exist.

216
00:07:53,580 --> 00:07:55,180
 In this scenario, a 500 is the wrong code,

217
00:07:55,180 --> 00:07:57,260
 because 500 says that the server is broken.

218
00:07:57,260 --> 00:07:58,500
 Meanwhile, the server is not broken.

219
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:00,300
 That can just set off an alarm for nothing.

220
00:08:00,300 --> 00:08:03,340
 The third mistake is mixing 401 and 403.

221
00:08:03,340 --> 00:08:06,140
 If you return 403 when the user's token has expired,

222
00:08:06,140 --> 00:08:08,520
 your front end doesn't send them back to the login,

223
00:08:08,520 --> 00:08:09,860
 which is what your front end should do.

224
00:08:09,860 --> 00:08:11,340
 So they just, they are stuck.

225
00:08:11,340 --> 00:08:15,060
 The first one is leaking stack traces in your 500 response.

226
00:08:15,060 --> 00:08:17,780
 In Lager, for example, if APP_DEBUG is true

227
00:08:17,780 --> 00:08:21,140
 and something crashes, the JSON error response

228
00:08:21,140 --> 00:08:23,580
 includes the exception message, your file path,

229
00:08:23,580 --> 00:08:25,900
 the line number, the full stack trace.

230
00:08:25,900 --> 00:08:27,900
 That's useful for you as the engineer.

231
00:08:27,900 --> 00:08:30,140
 In production, however, if you show that,

232
00:08:30,140 --> 00:08:31,620
 you are showing someone your folder structure

233
00:08:31,620 --> 00:08:32,460
 and now your code run.

234
00:08:32,460 --> 00:08:35,900
 So APP_DEBUG should always be forced in production,

235
00:08:35,900 --> 00:08:39,420
 with the body of your 500-response JOCI server error.

236
00:08:39,420 --> 00:08:41,020
 That's all your users need to know.

237
00:08:41,020 --> 00:08:44,960
 And one last HTTP status code, just for the fun of it.

238
00:08:44,960 --> 00:08:46,620
 418, I'm a teapot.

239
00:08:46,620 --> 00:08:47,460
 Bye.

